      "math_academy_id": "e-3242",
      "problem": "Find the value of $\\cos L.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224552/assets/78416d3c8ec8a8351ecfe0a4ea81feb05cce29f55dd9ec4bc676e6363dd49ec0.png)",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac {\\textrm{adjacent}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac {JL}{KL} = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{KL}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $KL.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nKL &= \\sqrt{ JK^2 + JL^2 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{ (3)^2 + (3\\sqrt3)^2 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{  9 + 27 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{36}\\\n&= 6\\\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\cos L &= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{KL} \\[5pt] \n&= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt 3}{6} \\[5pt] \n&=\\dfrac {\\sqrt 3} 2.\n}\n$$",
